update public.listening_clips set transcript_en = 'A: Good morning! Can I help you?
B: Yes, please. I want two rolls and one chocolate cake.
A: Anything else?
B: No, thank you. How much is it?
A: It''s twenty-two shekels.
B: Here you are.
A: Thank you! Have a nice day.
B: You too, bye!' where id = '175c1425-9675-4853-8c8e-0e0119d07818';
update public.listening_clips set transcript_en = 'B: Excuse me, where can I find the milk?
A: It is in aisle three, next to the eggs.
B: Thank you very much!
A: You are welcome, have a nice day.' where id = '8fbdc921-e9d0-47bd-9255-ba7a8d4d15f5';
update public.listening_clips set transcript_en = 'A: Hi Dan! Do you want to play basketball after school?
B: Yes! What time?
A: At four o''clock.
B: Where?
A: At the park near my house.
B: OK. Can my sister come too?
A: Sure! See you at four.' where id = '60e3fa82-3ebc-4d2a-832a-c7d9d8caebb1';
update public.listening_clips set transcript_en = 'B: Hi! My name is Tom. What''s your name?
A: I''m Maya.
B: Nice to meet you, Maya. Are you from here?
A: No, I''m from Eilat. I live in Tel Aviv now.
B: Is that your dog?
A: Yes, his name is Max. He is three years old. He loves the park!' where id = '12e5f3b7-7921-4301-8300-306599d1e1ad';
update public.listening_clips set transcript_en = 'A: Hi, I''m Dana. I just moved into the flat next door.
B: Oh, welcome! I''m Yossi. When did you arrive?
A: On Sunday. Everything is still in boxes.
B: I know how that feels. Do you need anything?
A: Actually, yes. Is there a supermarket near here?
B: Yes, there''s a small one at the end of the street. It''s open until ten at night. And the big one is about fifteen minutes away by car.
A: Thanks! And when is the rubbish collected?
B: On Mondays and Thursdays, early in the morning.
A: Good to know. Thanks for your help!' where id = 'b324e060-84c4-4ab7-8108-55b39f7ed4bb';
update public.listening_clips set transcript_en = 'A: Hi, can I help you?
B: Yes, I''m looking for a present for my brother. He''s turning fifteen.
A: What does he like?
B: He loves football and music.
A: We have these headphones. They''re very popular with teenagers.
B: How much are they?
A: They''re two hundred shekels.
B: Hmm, that''s a bit expensive. Do you have something cheaper?
A: This football shirt is one hundred and twenty.
B: Which team is it?
A: It''s the national team.
B: Perfect, he''ll love it. Can you wrap it for me?
A: Of course.' where id = 'c6bf2eec-445f-4118-b0a6-9beba0a53658';
update public.listening_clips set transcript_en = 'A: Good morning, Smile Dental Clinic.
B: Hello, I''d like to make an appointment, please. I have a toothache.
A: I''m sorry to hear that. Is it very bad?
B: It started two days ago, and it hurts when I drink something cold.
A: Okay. The doctor can see you tomorrow at eleven, or on Wednesday at four.
B: Tomorrow at eleven is fine.
A: Can I have your name, please?
B: It''s Ron Peretz.
A: Thank you, Ron. Please come ten minutes early and bring your ID card. See you tomorrow.' where id = '07b083a4-bd21-4510-a975-9fbef8ed062c';
update public.listening_clips set transcript_en = 'A: Hi, welcome to Sunny Burger, whaddya like to order?
B: Uh, yeah, can I get a cheeseburger and, um, a small fries?
A: You want a drink with that?
B: Sure, I''ll have a... actually, no, I''m good, just the burger and fries.
A: No problem, that''ll be seven fifty at the window.' where id = 'c05e51d6-2d95-40c0-a508-d3dbf1dc071c';
update public.listening_clips set transcript_en = 'B: Excuse me, is this the right platform for the train to Haifa?
A: Yes, it''s platform three, but the next train is late. It leaves at ten forty, not ten fifteen.
B: Oh no. Is there a café here?
A: Yes, there''s one near the main entrance, next to the ticket machines.
B: Thank you. And how long is the trip to Haifa?
A: About one hour and a half.
B: Great, thanks for your help.
A: You''re welcome. Have a good trip.' where id = 'ac7df226-8b22-4d69-aab2-3218c9ef0328';
update public.listening_clips set transcript_en = 'B: Mom, have you seen my keys? I can''t find them anywhere.
A: Did you look in your bag?
B: Yes, twice. They''re not there.
A: When did you last use them?
B: Yesterday evening, when I came home from the gym.
A: Then they''re probably in your jacket.
B: Which jacket?
A: The blue one. You wore it yesterday because it was cold.
B: Let me check. Yes! Here they are, in the left pocket.
A: Great. Now hurry, the bus leaves in ten minutes.
B: Thanks, Mom. See you tonight!' where id = 'cd39aec0-0d81-4086-b294-940de521b8a4';
update public.listening_clips set transcript_en = 'A: Good evening, City Taxis. How can I help you?
B: Hi, I need a taxi to the airport, please.
A: Sure, what time do you need it?
B: Six in the morning, if possible. My flight is at nine.
A: No problem, we will be there at six. What''s the address?
B: It''s twelve Herzl Street, near the post office.
A: And how many people are traveling?
B: Just me, but I have two big suitcases.
A: Okay, we''ll send a large car. The trip to the airport costs about one hundred and fifty shekels.
B: That''s fine. Can I pay by card?
A: Yes, all our drivers take cards.
B: Thank you!' where id = '82982e92-da94-4e5a-b29f-682750648a64';
update public.listening_clips set transcript_en = 'A: Hi Tom, are you free tomorrow afternoon for a quick meeting? I want to talk about the new website before we send it to the client.
B: Tomorrow? Let me check. I have something at two, but I am free after three.
A: Great, let''s meet at three thirty then. Should we meet in your office?
B: No, my office is too small. Let''s use the meeting room on the second floor.
A: Okay. Can you bring the photos for the home page?
B: Sure, I''ll bring them on my laptop. See you tomorrow.' where id = 'a64df234-a904-4df1-8681-dc1ed4694bb0';
update public.listening_clips set transcript_en = 'A: You look stressed. What''s wrong?
B: I totally missed my bus this morning. I was gonna be there by nine, but now I don''t know.
A: That sucks. Did you call them?
B: Yeah, I did, and they were like, "no big deal, just come when you can." So I guess it''s fine.
A: See, I told you they''re pretty chill about that stuff.
B: Yeah, I guess you''re right.' where id = '1207bfc0-8ed2-4b3c-8ceb-9207dfb690b4';
update public.listening_clips set transcript_en = 'A: Hey, you wanna grab lunch or what?
B: Um, I don''t know, I''m kinda swamped right now.
A: Come on, it''s just gonna take like twenty minutes.
B: Yeah... okay, fine. Where are we going, though?
A: I was thinking that new place on Main Street, the one with the... actually, never mind, let''s just go to the usual spot.
B: Sounds good. Gimme five minutes.' where id = '7aeac7d1-425e-45c3-bb43-7dec8e4850b8';
update public.listening_clips set transcript_en = 'A: Good morning, Green Street Clinic. How can I help?
B: Hi, I''d like to make an appointment with Doctor Levi, please.
A: Of course. Is it urgent?
B: Not really. I''ve had a sore throat for about a week and it''s not getting better.
A: I see. Doctor Levi''s first free appointment is on Monday at four fifteen.
B: Is there anything earlier?
A: I could do Friday morning with Doctor Cohen instead, at ten thirty.
B: Friday is better, thanks.
A: Fine. Can I have your ID number?
B: Sure, it''s on my card, one moment.
A: And please arrive ten minutes early, because we need to update your details.
B: No problem. Thank you.' where id = '62eac607-8325-4731-a1b9-23017b7b6b2d';
update public.listening_clips set transcript_en = 'A: Excuse me, can I talk to you for a moment?
B: Of course. Is everything all right?
A: Not really. I ordered this dish without onions, but there are onions in it. I''m allergic to them, so I can''t eat it.
B: I am so sorry about that, let me bring you a new plate right away. I''ll tell the kitchen myself so it doesn''t happen again.
A: Thank you, I appreciate it. Also, my friend''s soup is a bit cold.
B: Oh no, I''ll take that back too and bring a hot one. And because of the trouble, your desserts tonight are on us.
A: That''s very kind of you. Really, there''s no need.
B: Please, it''s the least we can do. Your food will be ready in about ten minutes.' where id = '91e5c2d1-1727-4afd-9694-eba23430ee5c';
update public.listening_clips set transcript_en = 'A: I was thinking we could go somewhere warm this winter. Maybe Eilat, or even Greece?
B: That sounds nice, but flights can be expensive during the holidays.
A: True, maybe we should book early to get a better price. My sister went to Crete last December and paid almost half because she booked in September.
B: Half? That''s a big difference. What about the hotel?
A: She stayed in a small apartment by the beach instead of a hotel. It had a kitchen, so they cooked most of their meals and saved even more.
B: I like that idea. But I don''t want to spend the whole vacation cooking.
A: Fair enough. We can cook breakfast and eat dinner out.
B: Okay, let''s look at flights tonight and decide by the weekend.' where id = '766f75ec-c569-4bed-97b0-79a9289dfd8e';
update public.listening_clips set transcript_en = 'A: So, which plan are you interested in?
B: I''m not sure. I use my phone mostly for messages and maps, and I watch videos on the train.
A: Then I''d recommend the basic plan with twenty gigabytes. It''s forty-nine shekels a month.
B: Is twenty enough if I watch videos every day?
A: Probably not. Video uses a lot of data. In that case, the unlimited plan is better. It''s sixty-nine shekels, and it includes calls abroad to thirty countries.
B: Is there a contract?
A: No, you can cancel at any time without paying anything extra. And if you join today, the first month is free.
B: That sounds good. I''ll take the unlimited plan, then.' where id = 'cbf2e56a-ad0c-4da2-8c2c-c9cc5a7c09f2';
update public.listening_clips set transcript_en = 'A: Do you think individuals can really make a difference in fighting climate change?
B: I believe small actions matter, but real change also requires policy shifts at the government level.
A: What do you mean by small actions?
B: Things like taking the train instead of flying for short trips, or eating less meat. On their own, they don''t solve much, but they change what people see as normal. And when enough people change their habits, politicians start to pay attention.
A: But isn''t that a bit optimistic? Most people won''t change unless it''s cheaper or easier.
B: That''s exactly why government policy matters. If public transport is fast and affordable, people use it without needing to be convinced.
A: So both are needed?
B: Exactly. Personal choices build pressure, and policy makes the better choice the easy one.' where id = '3f15512c-39c9-41d4-855f-2d71aa7f6f06';
update public.listening_clips set transcript_en = 'A: So, what are you up to this weekend?
B: Honestly? Not much. Maybe gonna catch up on sleep, watch a movie or something.
A: That''s it? I thought you were gonna go hiking with your brother.
B: Oh, right, yeah, that too — I kinda forgot about that, actually. He wants to leave super early, like six a.m., which is... not ideal.
A: Ha, good luck with that.' where id = '967fc368-e955-464f-b17b-7178e1fadbb7';
update public.listening_clips set transcript_en = 'A: Where are you? We''re supposed to start in like two minutes.
B: I know, I know, I''m so sorry, traffic''s insane right now. I''m gonna be there in ten, fifteen tops.
A: Ugh, okay, I''ll try to stall them.
B: You''re the best, I owe you one.
A: You owe me like five by now.' where id = '6f113830-2083-4058-b821-f68d41f83749';
update public.listening_clips set transcript_en = 'A: Thanks for holding. You''re through to customer support, this is Noa.
B: Hi. I bought a laptop from your website about three weeks ago, and the screen has started flickering. It happens every few minutes, and sometimes it goes completely black.
A: I''m sorry to hear that. Have you tried updating the graphics driver?
B: Yes, I did that on the first day it happened, and it made no difference.
A: Okay. Since you''re still within the thirty-day return period, you have two options. We can send you a prepaid label to return it for a full refund, or we can arrange a replacement, which would arrive in about five working days.
B: I''d prefer a replacement, but I need a computer for work. Can the new one be sent before I return this one?
A: Normally we wait for the return, but I can make an exception in this case. I''ll send the replacement today and give you two weeks to send the faulty one back.
B: That would be really helpful. Thank you.' where id = '6fcce422-6100-43d5-8382-0d749faaf5c6';
update public.listening_clips set transcript_en = 'A: I think school uniforms make a lot of sense. They save parents money, and nobody gets judged for not wearing expensive brands.
B: I see your point about money, but I''m not convinced it''s true in practice. Uniform shirts with the school logo often cost more than ordinary T-shirts, and kids still find ways to show off, with shoes, phones or bags.
A: Fair enough, but at least it reduces the pressure every single morning. Teenagers spend a ridiculous amount of time deciding what to wear.
B: Maybe, but choosing what to wear is also a way of expressing who you are. Taking that away at an age when you''re figuring out your identity doesn''t seem right to me.
A: So what''s the alternative?
B: A simple dress code, I''d say. No offensive slogans, nothing unsafe, and beyond that, let students decide for themselves.' where id = '89188675-8e05-4001-b796-44a35d29d681';
update public.listening_clips set transcript_en = 'A: Thanks for coming in today. Can you tell me about a challenge you faced at your previous job?
B: Sure. Last year we had a tight deadline on a project for one of our biggest clients. Two people on the team got sick in the same week, and we were about to miss the date.
A: What did you do?
B: First, I spoke to the client honestly and asked which features mattered most to them. Then I had to reorganize the team''s priorities to make sure we finished on time. We delivered the most important parts on the original date and the rest a week later.
A: How did the client react?
B: They were happy, actually. They said they preferred honesty to a last-minute surprise, and they gave us their next project too.
A: That''s a good example. What did you learn from it?
B: That a hard conversation early is better than an easy one too late.' where id = 'c2449137-9676-4a24-879d-221df387837c';
update public.listening_clips set transcript_en = 'A: Let''s move to the second question: what''s the real cost of remote work for companies?
B: Honestly, I think the biggest challenge isn''t productivity, it''s maintaining a sense of connection with your team when you rarely see them in person. Most studies show output stays roughly the same or even improves. But informal moments, like a chat after a meeting or asking a quick question across the desk, mostly disappear.
A: Why does that matter if the work still gets done?
B: Because that''s where trust is built, and where junior employees learn by watching more experienced colleagues. Companies that ignore that tend to see higher turnover, even if the numbers look fine on paper. People leave jobs where they feel like a name in a chat window.
A: So what do you recommend?
B: Not forcing everyone back five days a week, but planning regular days together with a clear purpose, so the time in the office is worth the commute.' where id = '9f292bf0-78c0-43ab-8b49-9f8f12ae9476';
update public.listening_clips set transcript_en = 'A: You worked in banking for years before becoming a teacher. What happened?
B: I''d been in finance for almost a decade before I realized it just wasn''t fulfilling anymore. The salary was good, but I was spending my days on reports that, honestly, nobody would remember a week later.
A: Was there a specific moment when you decided?
B: Not one dramatic moment, no. It was more a slow realization. I''d been volunteering on weekends, helping teenagers with maths, and I noticed I looked forward to Saturday mornings far more than to Monday meetings.
A: So you went back to study?
B: Yes, a two-year teaching course in the evenings while I was still working. It was exhausting. Making the switch was terrifying, but it''s the best decision I''ve made. Looking back, I''d say it''s easily the best one professionally. I earn less, but I go home knowing my work mattered to someone.' where id = '84744c42-d3e8-4115-a29b-113e975c808f';
update public.listening_clips set transcript_en = 'A: Many people worry that AI systems will make decisions no one can explain. Is that fear justified?
B: Partly. Some of the most capable systems are genuinely opaque, even to the people who built them. But I''d push back on the idea that human decisions are always transparent. A loan officer or a hiring manager can give you a reason, but it isn''t necessarily the real one. What matters, I''d argue, is accountability rather than perfect explainability. If a system affects someone''s life, there should be a named person or institution responsible for it, a way to challenge the outcome, and regular checks for bias.
A: So you''re not calling for a ban?
B: Not across the board, no. Bans make sense in a few narrow areas. Elsewhere, the harder and more useful work is building institutions that can say no when a system gets it wrong.' where id = 'cd62a3e0-580d-4c19-9755-f9313cc59b6d';

